WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.110
Language immersion.

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.820
It's a term we hear all the time in
connection with learning languages.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.500
I've talked a bit about it before.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.060
I wanna talk about it now with
reference to my recent visit to Poland.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.280
So, you know, we have language
immersion programs in school.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:21.330
My grandchildren here in Vancouver
did all their schooling in French,

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.100
so they are immersed in French.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:24.780
The teacher speaks in French.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.850
All the subjects are covered in French.

00:00:27.975 --> 00:00:31.335
As a result of that, they get a
fairly high level of comprehension.

00:00:31.365 --> 00:00:36.225
Their speaking ability isn't necessarily
all that great, however, it is ready

00:00:36.225 --> 00:00:39.255
to be activated at any time because
they have been immersed in the

00:00:39.255 --> 00:00:40.935
language, they have good comprehension.

00:00:41.535 --> 00:00:43.725
They have learned many
subjects using French.

00:00:43.785 --> 00:00:45.465
That's one form of immersion.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:51.060
You also hear people say, you know, to,
you have to, to learn the language, you

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.860
need to immerse yourself in the language.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.420
It's a good image: you kind of
get into the pool, you get wet.

00:00:57.425 --> 00:00:59.190
You're sort of drowning in the language.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:00.480
You're immersed in the language.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.879
But what does it really mean?

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.300
Uh, obviously if you go to a
country where you can't speak

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.039
the language or you've ha...

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.370
or you have a very limited knowledge
of the language, it's very difficult

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:12.840
to be immersed in the language.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.250
You are surrounded by the language,
you're surrounded by people who speak

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:23.190
the language, you may hear the language
on radio or television, but you are

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.950
not really connected to the language.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.440
You're not participating in the language.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:33.660
And so many people can live in a
country where a language is spoken,

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.070
and if they don't put any effort
into learning the language, being

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:41.940
there alone, being immersed in the
language is not gonna do much for them.

00:01:42.855 --> 00:01:47.535
By the same token, you can immerse
yourself in the language without being in

00:01:47.535 --> 00:01:49.335
the country where the language is spoken.

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:52.665
You can read, you can listen,
you can talk to people online.

00:01:52.875 --> 00:01:57.105
You can create sort of a language
world for yourself, which

00:01:57.105 --> 00:01:59.025
is also a form of immersion.

00:01:59.745 --> 00:02:02.895
Now, how does all that
relate to my visit to Poland?

00:02:04.005 --> 00:02:08.685
So I came back, I was in Poland
for I guess six or seven days.

00:02:08.775 --> 00:02:09.975
Really enjoyed it.

00:02:10.005 --> 00:02:11.955
I enjoyed discovering Poland.

00:02:13.214 --> 00:02:17.625
Uh, my wife and I flew first to
Warsaw, then turned around and

00:02:17.625 --> 00:02:19.035
the next day went to Copenhagen.

00:02:19.035 --> 00:02:21.494
That's just the way our
ticket was structured.

00:02:21.825 --> 00:02:26.144
And, uh, then we worked our way
back to Poland through Sweden, and

00:02:26.149 --> 00:02:31.785
basically from the evening of the
25th until the, you know, midday of

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:34.785
the 2nd of June, we were in Poland.

00:02:35.445 --> 00:02:37.964
Half of the time, roughly spent in Warsaw.

00:02:37.964 --> 00:02:44.775
Half of it spent in Kraków which is, uh,
one of the historical capitals of Poland.

00:02:45.435 --> 00:02:49.875
Now, since I was there with my wife,
mostly we were speaking English.

00:02:50.325 --> 00:02:56.655
My attempts at speaking Polish despite
having spent two months prior to going,

00:02:57.115 --> 00:03:01.635
you know, listening to a lot of Polish,
reading, a lot of Polish, I had one

00:03:02.005 --> 00:03:09.480
italki lesson before going, uh, that's
not really immersion, but it's a start

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.490
towards immersing myself in the language.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:16.410
Uh, eight years ago I had a program
of three months where I did the same.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:23.790
So, and I had committed to doing two
interviews in Polish when in Poland.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.610
And these took place in, uh, Kraków.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:32.700
So prior to going, I had attempted
to use my Polish on the flight,

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:38.010
going over, uh, with taxi drivers,
with very limited success.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:46.769
I was very much aware of my limitations,
and yet arriving in Kraków on the 29th,

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.470
I had an interview entirely in Polish.

00:03:49.875 --> 00:03:52.545
For Easy Polish, uh, a website.

00:03:52.545 --> 00:03:54.554
I'll leave a link in the description box.

00:03:55.515 --> 00:03:59.084
Uh, I am in fact following up
with them to see if I can get at

00:03:59.084 --> 00:04:02.204
least a part of that interview
and put it up here on my channel.

00:04:02.204 --> 00:04:08.114
So you can see me struggling in a
combination of Slavic languages, lot

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:11.204
of mistakes, however, communicating.

00:04:11.864 --> 00:04:14.954
So the important thing there is
that for, I think it was 10 or

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:16.515
15 minutes, maybe it was longer.

00:04:17.145 --> 00:04:22.905
I was communicating in Polish, so I was
hearing polish, I was hearing polish

00:04:22.965 --> 00:04:27.315
having to do with the subject that
we were discussing, and so therefore,

00:04:27.315 --> 00:04:29.565
that input is very relevant to me.

00:04:29.565 --> 00:04:34.940
It's what I call very high resonance
because I'm very much involved in it.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:38.565
I can't be wandering, my attention
can't be wandering somewhere else.

00:04:38.565 --> 00:04:42.465
I have to pay attention to what people
are saying, so I'm getting that input.

00:04:42.765 --> 00:04:44.745
I'm making mistakes while speaking.

00:04:45.224 --> 00:04:49.185
Um, aware of some of the
mistakes as I'm making them.

00:04:49.724 --> 00:04:52.815
Uh, if I see a video of the
interview, I will be aware

00:04:52.815 --> 00:04:54.734
of more mistakes that I make.

00:04:55.125 --> 00:04:59.385
And all of this, it does kind
of nudge me in the direction of

00:04:59.385 --> 00:05:03.284
speaking better because I start
to notice things in the language.

00:05:03.705 --> 00:05:08.085
Uh, maybe I'm, you know, I'm
aware of many rules that I

00:05:08.085 --> 00:05:10.155
can't really apply on the fly.

00:05:10.695 --> 00:05:15.045
Uh, but then I notice when I see myself
in a video that I made this or that

00:05:15.045 --> 00:05:18.765
mistake, I know it's a mistake, but
in the heat of the moment speaking, I

00:05:18.765 --> 00:05:21.075
couldn't produce the language correctly.

00:05:21.495 --> 00:05:23.675
But that is a form of, of immersion.

00:05:24.705 --> 00:05:32.125
Uh, the next day I had another interview
uh, with a gentleman, Maciej Wieczorek,

00:05:32.354 --> 00:05:34.275
who is a motivational speaker in Poland.

00:05:34.784 --> 00:05:39.645
He does these interviews in his
Bentley, which is his studio, and we

00:05:39.645 --> 00:05:43.005
drive around the city and he asks me
questions and we spoke in a number

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:47.534
of languages including Polish, and
that will also go up on his channel.

00:05:47.534 --> 00:05:51.195
I will, at some point make
you aware of where that, you

00:05:51.195 --> 00:05:52.185
know, where you can see that.

00:05:53.085 --> 00:05:56.474
Again, I struggled in my Polish, I
struggled in some of the other languages.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.980
Again, it's a form of immersion.

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:02.490
Even if you're doing poorly, it's
a form of immersion because you are

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:04.590
involved in speaking with someone.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:10.290
But I think also, like on my way back,
first of all, we had a meetup in Warsaw

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:15.820
and one of the at attendants was an
American will who lives in Gdańsk.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:24.270
And uh, he gave me this book, which is
a story told by someone describing life

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:32.159
in Poland, uh, before the outbreak of
the Second World War, and a very kind

00:06:32.159 --> 00:06:33.900
of, well, he came down from Gdańsk.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:42.120
And he even wrote a little, you know,
uh, dedication message here, thanking

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:47.070
me for the influence that I have had on
his attitude towards language learning.

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:51.645
A very positive person is Will and
I thank him very much for the book.

00:06:51.975 --> 00:06:54.525
I picked up a lot of books in Poland.

00:06:55.065 --> 00:06:57.555
I can never resist going to bookstores.

00:06:57.555 --> 00:06:58.635
I bought a bunch of books.

00:06:58.635 --> 00:07:01.815
My luggage, luggage was
much heavier going back.

00:07:02.235 --> 00:07:10.295
I've also found audiobooks and eBooks
on history, uh, from publio, uh,

00:07:10.315 --> 00:07:12.355
.pl, which I've imported into LingQ.

00:07:12.375 --> 00:07:14.085
I've been reading them.

00:07:14.355 --> 00:07:18.465
I also did find the digital
version of this book.

00:07:18.735 --> 00:07:22.695
And so I've been doing that on
LingQ alternating between reading

00:07:22.695 --> 00:07:24.825
it online and reading it on paper.

00:07:25.455 --> 00:07:29.594
Uh, this book, I couldn't find any
digital version of it, but I read the

00:07:29.594 --> 00:07:33.164
whole thing on the plane coming back.

00:07:33.164 --> 00:07:38.115
And I've often said that when you read
a, a paper book away from the internet,

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:43.230
The whole book, the, your first book is
like a major milestone in the language.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:48.900
So all of the things that, and, and
to me this is a form of, of immersion.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:54.840
Uh, it takes a long time for this
passive vocabulary to, to crystallize, to

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:59.595
activate, to where you're actually able
to use this, and you do eventually have to

00:08:00.015 --> 00:08:05.625
put yourself into situations where you're
using these words all the time, but you

00:08:05.625 --> 00:08:09.735
are really building up your capability,
you're developing your comprehension.

00:08:09.885 --> 00:08:15.285
It is a form of immersion, just
like, uh, kids like my grandchildren

00:08:15.285 --> 00:08:17.025
in French immersion in Canada.

00:08:17.235 --> 00:08:18.525
It's mostly passive.

00:08:18.525 --> 00:08:19.925
It's mostly hearing the language.

00:08:20.265 --> 00:08:23.924
It's mostly learning things
through the language, but I also

00:08:23.924 --> 00:08:26.174
consider that a form of immersion.

00:08:26.625 --> 00:08:30.284
Uh, it's just simply not
practical always to speak.

00:08:30.344 --> 00:08:35.265
Speaking is one form of immersion, largely
because of the input that you get back.

00:08:35.775 --> 00:08:38.595
Uh, but listening and
reading is also immersion.

00:08:38.985 --> 00:08:44.280
Visiting Poland, seeing Kraków,
interacting even in a limited way with

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:48.690
Polish people, seeing how they live,
eating their food, getting a sense of

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:53.490
their history, traveling in the country,
traveling on their trains, uh, having

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:55.810
a coffee on the square in Kraków.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:01.320
All of these things are immersing
you in the language, the culture, the

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:03.510
history, the environment of the language.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:05.310
It's all part of immersion.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:09.330
So immersion is not just sitting
down with someone and speaking.

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:14.580
Immersion is getting involved with
everything that relates to that

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:18.900
language, building up your passive
capability, which is going to form the

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:21.690
base then for your active capability.

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:27.010
So I think the definition of immersion
should be much broader than just going

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:30.150
to a country and assuming you can
start speaking to people, you can't.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:34.140
There's so many things, so many
ways you can immerse yourself in

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:37.950
the language before going so that
when you do go there, you can get

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:43.230
greater advantage from the potential
immersion opportunities that are there.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:48.210
Uh, I am determined to continue with
my reading and passive immersion

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:53.580
activities on LingQ where I have a
number of books on, uh, Polish history.

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:57.120
And it's good to do something like
history using different resources.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:59.820
Just like with the language,
you wanna cover the same ground

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:01.530
using different resources.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:08.640
And I think that, uh, already now, I think
my capability to speak would be better

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:11.580
than it was when I arrived in Kraków.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:17.010
And if I give myself another few weeks
here with Polish history and, and other

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:21.660
resources like this book here, which
I have both on LingQ to look up the

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:26.610
words and reading it on paper to have
a different kind of experience, um,

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:28.500
I think my capability will improve.

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:34.170
And, uh, I will try to schedule a
discussion, another discussion in Polish

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:40.350
in two weeks to a month as I continue
along in my immersion program in Polish.

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:44.430
And the final thing was I
was very surprised by Poland.

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:47.290
Very positive country,
uh, energetic people.

00:10:48.360 --> 00:10:50.490
A lot of things work very well.

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:53.819
Many people out on the streets in
the evenings enjoying themselves.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:55.020
We had a great time.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:56.730
Thank you for listening.

00:10:56.819 --> 00:10:57.390
Bye for now.

